1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,320
Give one Grok Bot research.

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:05,480
Give another editing. Sounds like a team.

3
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:08,960
But here is the engineering detail I would check first: what

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:12,640
do they share? According to the official documentation, Bots

5
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:15,840
on your account share a computer, including files, signed-in

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,720
browser sessions, and command-line credentials.

7
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:20,773
Each Bot has its own screen.

8
00:00:20,827 --> 00:00:23,840
That does not make each one a separate security boundary.

9
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:25,680
That gives you two questions.

10
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,720
Permission: may this actor change the resource?

11
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:32,080
Coordination: which authorized actor owns the change right

12
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:35,680
now? A role name alone does not answer either one.

13
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:39,040
In my independent TypeScript example, a worker pauses.

14
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:42,880
Its claim expires. Another worker saves a reviewed report.

15
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:44,800
Then the old worker comes back.

16
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:48,400
Its late write gets refused, so the reviewed result survives.

17
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:51,227
The example has twenty-three passing tests.

18
00:00:51,253 --> 00:00:54,400
It is an in-memory teaching tool, not a Grok Bot integration

19
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:56,040
or a product benchmark.

20
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:58,880
The practical lesson is to make ownership and version checks

21
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:02,720
explicit. Before you add another AI teammate, decide what they

22
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,360
share, who owns the result, and who gets the last write.

23
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:09,920
Read the full article on my blog at B H J R dot dev.
